  "guideTopicSummary": "{topic, select, scarification{Removes thatch and moss, lets the turf breathe} aeration{Loosens compacted soil so water and fertilizer reach the roots} raking{Gently removes dead grass, leaves and thin thatch} overseeding{Thickens the lawn and fills bare spots} sanding{Loosens heavy soil and levels the surface} liming{Reduces soil acidity when pH drops too low} other{}}",
  "guideTopicWhen": "{topic, select, scarification{April–May, optionally late August–September} aeration{April–May or September} raking{March–April and in autumn, with leaves} overseeding{Late August–September, April–May} sanding{Right after aeration, in spring or September} liming{October–November, when pH is below 5.5} other{}}",
  "guideTopicWhenText": "{topic, select, scarification{When the grass is growing strongly and the soil is at least 10°C. Usually after the 2nd or 3rd spring mow. Not in heat and not on wet soil.} aeration{When the grass is growing and will quickly cover the holes. The soil should be moist but not wet: tines go in easily and don't smear it.} raking{In spring, once the lawn dries after winter, and in autumn when leaves fall. It is gentler than scarifying, so you can do it more often.} overseeding{Best from late August to mid-September: the soil is warm and dew keeps it moist. In spring, once the soil is at least 8–10°C.} sanding{Best right after aeration or scarification, while the holes are open. The lawn and sand must be dry.} liming{In autumn, after the last fertilizing, or in early spring. Only when a test shows acidic soil: grass grows best at pH 5.5–7.} other{}}",
  "guideTopicSigns": "{topic, select, scarification{There is a springy layer of thatch over 1 cm under the blades\nMoss grows between the grass\nThe lawn responds poorly to fertilizer and water} aeration{Water stands on the lawn long after rain\nThe soil is hard, a screwdriver barely goes in\nGrass thins on paths and play areas} raking{After winter the grass is matted and dry\nLeaves and clippings lie on the lawn\nThe thatch is thin, under 1 cm} overseeding{The lawn is thinning and soil shows\nWinter or scarifying left bare patches\nWeeds grow in the gaps} sanding{The soil is heavy clay and compacted\nWater stands long after rain\nThe lawn has small bumps} liming{A pH test showed less than 5.5\nMoss is spreading in the lawn\nThe grass responds poorly to fertilizer} other{}}",
  "guideTopicSteps": "{topic, select, scarification{Mow low: To 3–4 cm, a day or two before. The lawn must be dry.\nSet the blades shallow: They should cut 2–3 mm into the soil. Deeper damages the roots.\nGo along, then across: At a steady pace, without stopping in one place.\nRake up the thatch: There can be surprisingly much. The lawn looks worse for 2 weeks, that's normal.\nReseed and fertilize: Reseed bare spots right away, then apply spring fertilizer.\nWater: Regularly for 2–3 weeks if it doesn't rain.} aeration{Mow the lawn: To the usual height, a day before.\nWater if dry: The day before, so the soil softens.\nPierce the soil: With a hollow-tine aerator to 6–10 cm, about every 10 cm.\nLeave the cores: They dry in 1–2 days, then break them up with a rake.\nFill the holes with sand: Sanding right after aeration works best.\nFertilize and water: Nutrients go straight to the roots.} raking{Wait until the lawn dries: Wet turf is easily pulled out with the roots.\nRake with a fan rake: Firmly but shallow, in two directions.\nCollect the debris: Put leaves and dead grass on the compost.\nReseed gaps: Reseed winter bare spots when the soil is 8–10°C.} overseeding{Prepare the soil: Rake or scarify the lawn and loosen bare spots.\nSow the seed: 15–20 g/m², on bare spots 25–35 g/m². Sow in two directions.\nCover lightly: About 0.5 cm of soil or sand, then roll gently.\nWater daily: Gently, a little at a time, for 2–3 weeks until the grass comes up.\nFirst mow: When the young grass is 8–10 cm, cut it to 5–6 cm.} sanding{Aerate first: The sand should fall into the tine holes.\nSpread the sand: 3–5 l/m², a layer of 3–5 mm. Washed sand, 0–2 mm grain.\nSpread and brush in: With the back of a rake or a brush until the sand disappears into the turf.\nWater: Water rinses the remaining sand off the blades.} liming{Measure pH: With a meter or strips, in several places.\nChoose the dose: Calcium carbonate or dolomite lime, usually 10–20 kg per 100 m². The label gives the exact dose.\nSpread evenly: With a spreader, in calm weather, in two directions.\nWater or wait for rain: The lime has to get into the soil.\nCheck pH after a year: Don't lime every season without testing.} other{}}",
  "guideTopicTools": "{topic, select, scarification{Scarifier\nRake\nOverseeding seed\nSpring fertilizer} aeration{Hollow-tine aerator\nRake\nWashed sand} raking{Fan rake\nPowered lawn rake (optional)\nBags or compost bin} overseeding{Seed of the same mix\nRake\nSoil or sand for covering\nRoller (optional)} sanding{Washed sand 0–2 mm\nRake or brush\nWheelbarrow and shovel} liming{pH meter or test strips\nCalcium carbonate or dolomite lime\nSpreader} other{}}",
  "guideTopicAvoid": "{topic, select, scarification{A lawn younger than 2 years from seed or 1 year from sod. Scarifying in heat, drought or on wet soil. More than 1–2 times a year.} aeration{A lawn less than a year old. Aerating dry, hard soil or during drought. Solid tines on heavy soil only compact it. On clay 1–2 times a year is enough, on sand even less.} raking{Raking a wet or frozen lawn. Leaves left over winter: the grass under them rots and gets diseases.} overseeding{Sowing in heat, drought or into cold soil. Walking on fresh seed. Weed killers for 6–8 weeks before and after sowing.} sanding{Builder's sand with clay and fine dusty sand. A layer over 5 mm at once: the grass will suffocate. On sandy soil sanding is usually not needed.} liming{Lime together with nitrogen fertilizer: leave 3–4 weeks between them. Quicklime, which burns grass. Liming without a pH test.} other{}}",
  "guideMonthTitle": "{month, select, jan{Winter: the lawn rests} feb{Getting ready for the season} mar{Raking and first feed} apr{Scarification and aeration} may{Overseeding and regular mowing} jun{Mowing and watering} jul{Heat: mow higher} aug{Overseeding late in the month} sep{Aeration, sanding, overseeding} oct{Liming and last mowing} nov{Raking leaves} dec{Winter: the lawn rests} other{}}",
  "guideMonthText": "{month, select, jan{Don't walk on frozen grass and plan your spring jobs.} feb{Sharpen the mower blades and check your equipment before spring.} mar{Once the lawn dries, rake out thatch and leaves. Apply a starter fertilizer late in the month.} apr{Grass starts growing. It's the best time for treatments and reseeding bare spots.} may{Last chance for spring scarification. Mow every 4–7 days.} jun{Water less often but more deeply, preferably in the morning.} jul{Raise the mower by 1 cm and don't apply nitrogen in heat.} aug{When the heat passes, the best time for overseeding begins.} sep{Warm soil and dew help the lawn recover quickly after treatments.} oct{Autumn is a good time to reduce soil acidity before winter.} nov{Don't leave leaves over winter. Last mow to about 5 cm.} dec{Don't walk on frozen grass and don't pile salty snow on it.} other{}}",
